<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neutral Confederacy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neutral Nation, Attawandaron, Chonnonton</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neutral Confederacy was a confederation of Iroquoian peoples in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, along the Grand River and the north shore of Lake Erie to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a>. The French called them Neutral because they tried to stay out of the wars between the Wendat and the Haudenosaunee, though they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mascouten">Mascouten</a>, or Fire Nation. Skilled hunters, they traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and deerskins to the Wendat for European goods, and their flint deposits let them trade with both rival confederacies. In 1641, after an epidemic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> counted 40 villages and about 12,000 people. After Haudenosaunee attacks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> they dispersed in 1651 and joined other Iroquoian nations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Beaver Wars the league fought for control of furs and captives: in 1649 Haudenosaunee war parties destroyed the main towns of the Huron Confederacy and dispersed its people, then broke the <b>Neutral Confederacy</b> around 1651 and the Erie Nation by about 1656, absorbing thousands of captives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
